1
00:00:00,075 --> 00:00:01,123
Hey, what's going on friends?

2
00:00:01,123 --> 00:00:07,858
So today we are answering a question that I've gotten asked quite a few times over the years, which is what is the best format to read books in?

3
00:00:07,858 --> 00:00:14,788
Physical books, eBooks, or audiobooks, where I guess you aren't actually reading the book, but you are still intaking it.

4
00:00:14,788 --> 00:00:22,802
Well, I'm gonna save you the horse race here because in my experience, I use all three formats and all three of these can be perfectly viable ways to intake a book.

5
00:00:22,802 --> 00:00:30,307
But depending on how you are reading and the purpose of your reading, one of the three might have a slight edge over the others.

6
00:00:30,307 --> 00:00:32,413
So that's kind of what I wanna go into in this video.

7
00:00:32,413 --> 00:00:36,716
And I'm not gonna break this video down by the format itself and section out that way.

8
00:00:36,716 --> 00:00:43,901
[4 reasons why we read] Instead, I wanna look at the purpose for which we read, because I think there are four main purposes for why we pick up a book.

9
00:00:43,901 --> 00:00:46,234
First and foremost, we sometimes read for relaxation.

10
00:00:46,234 --> 00:00:47,311
We read for escapism.

11
00:00:47,311 --> 00:00:49,345
We just want to have fun through reading.

12
00:00:49,345 --> 00:00:54,128
We may also read to widen our learning, our basic knowledge, or to widen our T.

13
00:00:54,128 --> 00:01:02,552
If you wanna think about it that way, the concept of a T-shaped person where you have deep knowledge in one area, but then wide, expansive knowledge in many other areas as well.

14
00:01:02,552 --> 00:01:06,064
You may also read for deep learning to deeply understand a topic.

15
00:01:06,064 --> 00:01:14,471
And finally, you might read for reference, for research, for a specific purpose where you're trying to pull knowledge out of a book and apply it to something you're trying to do.

16
00:01:14,471 --> 00:01:17,681
So for each of these four different purposes, what is the best format to choose?

17
00:01:17,681 --> 00:01:20,911
Well, again, I think it really comes down to what you enjoy using.

18
00:01:20,911 --> 00:01:23,607
All the edges that we're gonna be talking here are the advantages.

19
00:01:23,607 --> 00:01:25,603
I think they're slight for the most part.

20
00:01:25,603 --> 00:01:30,194
And ultimately, reading and the format you choose to read in is just like exercise.

21
00:01:30,194 --> 00:01:35,472
There's no perfect routine because the best overall routine is the one that you enjoy and the one that you will stick to.

22
00:01:35,472 --> 00:01:40,405
[Reading for Fun] For the first purpose, for reading, for escapism, and for fun, what is the best format?

23
00:01:40,405 --> 00:01:44,004
Well, I think in general, the best format here is physical books.

24
00:01:44,004 --> 00:01:45,829
There's some actual research behind this.

25
00:01:45,829 --> 00:01:50,368
Now, the first study that we looked at for this video was albeit pretty small in terms of sample size...

26
00:01:50,368 --> 00:01:58,479
...but they did find that versus eBooks, people who read physical books were actually better able to remember the order in which things happened in a story.

27
00:01:58,479 --> 00:02:09,018
There might be something to the physical tactile sensations you get through reading a book, the act of turning a page, the act of remembering which side of the book you were looking at when something happened,

28
00:02:09,018 --> 00:02:17,280
that allows you to have a little bit better memory of what went on than when you're just looking at an iPad screen or a Kindle screen, just scrolling through,

29
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:21,823
in my case, a never-ending wall of text because I typically use the scrolling mode on the Kindle app.

30
00:02:21,823 --> 00:02:30,539
Another potential downside to eBooks in particular is the fact that we often read them on tablets, and even the Kindle devices have a web browser built in, albeit not a very good one.

31
00:02:30,539 --> 00:02:43,106
And when we have access to additional information, additional things we can do with our device, it gets a little bit harder to really sink into the world of the book and to really feel like you are reading for fun, to get lost in the story.

32
00:02:43,106 --> 00:02:46,667
Now, what about audiobooks for fun reading, for escapism?

33
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:50,452
There are quite a few audiobooks I've actually gone through in terms of fiction that I've really enjoyed...

34
00:02:50,452 --> 00:02:55,534
...specifically the one that keeps coming to mind for me while I film this video is *The Martian* by Andy Weir...

35
00:02:55,534 --> 00:03:01,309
...and I have the old R.C. Bray-narrated version that they no longer have on Audible, which is absolutely amazing.

36
00:03:01,309 --> 00:03:09,618
But I also know that I typically listen to audiobooks when I'm doing things, when I'm riding my bike or walking to work or doing chores around the house.

37
00:03:09,618 --> 00:03:15,101
And because I'm also doing something else that takes a little bit of my attention, not a ton, but a little bit...

38
00:03:15,101 --> 00:03:17,493
...there are gonna be pieces that I just sort of tune out for.

39
00:03:17,493 --> 00:03:23,142
I'm gonna have to navigate a road crossing or something, and that means I'm not getting every single bit of the story and I'm not totally immersed.

40
00:03:23,142 --> 00:03:28,310
So again, I think the edge really goes to paper books for escapism-style reading.

41
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:31,973
[Reading for KNOWLEDGE] Now, what about widening your T, widening your base of knowledge?

42
00:03:31,973 --> 00:03:44,280
Well, like I said earlier, the concept of being a T-shaped person means that you've built deep expertise in at least one area, but then you're also reading to widen your array of knowledge across a broad variety of topics.

43
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:51,168
And for doing that, again, these are small edges we're talking about here, but I actually am personally going to give the edge to audiobooks.

44
00:03:51,168 --> 00:03:59,101
And that is because for this purpose, for just trying to widen your base of knowledge, that need for complete immersion is less important.

45
00:03:59,101 --> 00:04:00,738
And I'm a busy guy.

46
00:04:00,738 --> 00:04:03,611
I've got a lot of things I have to do around the house a lot of time.

47
00:04:03,611 --> 00:04:04,963
I have to walk to work every single day.

48
00:04:04,963 --> 00:04:10,811
There's time throughout the day where I have to do something where my attention isn't completely taken up.

49
00:04:10,811 --> 00:04:15,168
And I might otherwise just listen to podcasts or Spotify or just find something to listen to on YouTube...

50
00:04:15,168 --> 00:04:23,130
...but I can also select a book and I can broaden my knowledge on something in a pretty shallow way that's still interesting and enlightening using an audiobook.

51
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:28,068
For example, I've been riding my bike to work over the past few months, listening to Bill Bryson's book on the human body.

52
00:04:28,068 --> 00:04:33,318
And I don't feel that I need a complete and super deep understanding of all these different structures.

53
00:04:33,318 --> 00:04:35,430
I just wanna learn for learning's sake.

54
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:38,129
So the audiobook format is perfect for that purpose.

55
00:04:38,129 --> 00:04:44,663
Now, aside from audiobooks, I don't think there's a huge edge between eBooks and physical books when it comes to this type of learning.

56
00:04:44,663 --> 00:04:50,254
I might give a slight nod to eBooks simply because it is typically easier to get a new book on a whim.

57
00:04:50,254 --> 00:04:54,703
And there are even library programs that will let you borrow eBooks on your electronic devices.

58
00:04:54,703 --> 00:04:58,545
So it's just easier to get your hands on an eBook when you want to read something on a whim.

59
00:04:58,545 --> 00:05:05,179
[Reading for Research&Reference] Now, one area where I definitely think eBooks have the clear advantage is when you're reading for the purposes of research and reference...

60
00:05:05,179 --> 00:05:11,845
...when you're trying to pull knowledge out of a book and actually do something with it, synthesize the information and write a book report.

61
00:05:11,845 --> 00:05:16,954
Or in my case, the huge video that I'm working on for later this month is an ultimate guide to using credit cards.

62
00:05:16,954 --> 00:05:25,409
I've been trying to pull information out of several different sources, including books, and synthesize it into something that I can present to this channel and present it to my blog as well.

63
00:05:25,409 --> 00:05:31,346
So I need to pull accurate information, I need to collect it, and I think eBooks definitely make this process easier...

64
00:05:31,346 --> 00:05:34,213
...not least of which because you can search an eBook.

65
00:05:34,213 --> 00:05:38,438
You can easily bring up a little search field and you can type in a term and see where it pops up in the book.

66
00:05:38,438 --> 00:05:42,668
Additionally, highlighting and taking notes is quite a bit easier with eBooks.

67
00:05:42,668 --> 00:05:50,226
On the Kindle app, for example, you can just use your finger and create a highlight, and those highlights actually get collected in a centralized location within the book.

68
00:05:50,226 --> 00:05:53,200
Whereas with a paper book, there are definitely ways to highlight.

69
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:57,055
I made an entire video about how I highlight and take notes from books that you can watch after this one...

70
00:05:57,055 --> 00:06:00,550
...but it's kind of harder to go find those highlights.

71
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:07,652
You don't know where they are, you can't search them, and it's a little bit harder to collect the information from them than it is with, say, a Kindle book.

72
00:06:07,652 --> 00:06:15,030
Additionally, you can go even further than simply highlighting in the Kindle app or in the iBooks app because there is an app that I use every day called Readwise.

73
00:06:15,030 --> 00:06:24,112
And what Readwise does is it basically just collects your highlights from Kindle books, but also from articles, from tweets, from certain podcast apps, and it keeps them in a central repository.&nbsp;

74
00:06:24,112 --> 00:06:32,114
And it goes one step further because they have a public API where you can export your highlights to apps like Obsidian, Roam, and in my case, Notion.

75
00:06:32,114 --> 00:06:39,501
So I use Readwise for the express purpose of getting my highlights from Kindle books and from other sources into a Notion database.

76
00:06:39,501 --> 00:06:49,159
From there, I can add notes to them and I can even use synced blocks to bring those highlights and those little passages that I'm using for research into the video script and research areas that I'm working on.

77
00:06:49,159 --> 00:06:50,683
Now, to be fair to paper books...

78
00:06:50,683 --> 00:06:57,796
...there is also a Readwise app for mobile devices where you can actually take a picture of the printed page and it has that optical character recognition technology.&nbsp;

79
00:06:57,796 --> 00:07:00,805
So you can actually create a Readwise highlight from a paper book...

80
00:07:00,805 --> 00:07:07,132
...but it is a little bit of a slower and more friction-full process than just creating a very quick highlight in the Kindle app.

81
00:07:07,132 --> 00:07:15,245
[for DEEP Understanding] And that brings us to our final purpose for reading, which is reading for deep understanding, as opposed to that wide understanding that builds the horizontal part of your T.

82
00:07:15,245 --> 00:07:19,667
We're talking now about that vertical bar, building a really deep understanding of a specific topic.

83
00:07:19,667 --> 00:07:25,936
And here, I think the edge actually goes back to paper books specifically because they make it so much easier to focus...

84
00:07:25,936 --> 00:07:29,126
...not get distracted by whatever else is on your device.

85
00:07:29,126 --> 00:07:33,519
It's just a little bit easier to maintain focus on a paper book, at least in my experience...

86
00:07:33,519 --> 00:07:41,063
...than it is on an eBook when you're trying to read for deep understanding and not necessarily trying to pull out references for a research project.

87
00:07:41,063 --> 00:07:43,080
Though there is a potential alternative.

88
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:47,919
So recently, a friend gifted me the eBook version of Alex Hormozi's *$100 Million Offers*.

89
00:07:47,919 --> 00:07:58,730
And right in the first chapter, he mentions that a trick he uses is actually getting the audiobook version and listening to it while following along in the print or the eBook version as well.

90
00:07:58,730 --> 00:08:04,559
And he says it actually helps him read faster because it helps him to pay attention to the eBook for a lot longer.

91
00:08:04,559 --> 00:08:11,082
Now, I do want to mention that if you do this, it's probably not going to make you read faster from an objective sense.

92
00:08:11,082 --> 00:08:15,552
And that is because audiobooks are typically narrated at about an average of 150 words per minute...

93
00:08:15,552 --> 00:08:23,954
...whereas skilled readers who are reading silently to themselves and not subvocalizing can typically read between 200 and 400 words per minute without losing a lot of comprehension.

94
00:08:23,954 --> 00:08:33,830
So unless you want to set the audiobook to 2X speed, which does not sound very pleasant to me, you're probably gonna end up actually reading a little bit slower than you would if you were just silently reading to yourself.

95
00:08:33,830 --> 00:08:37,853
But if the focus benefit is there, it might be a useful strategy for you.

96
00:08:37,853 --> 00:08:50,365
Now, one thing I want to mention that doesn't really fit into any of these specific categories is that eBooks do have a clear advantage over many print books in the fact that they are more accessible because you can easily change the font size, you can switch things into dark mode, you have a lot of options there.

97
00:08:50,365 --> 00:08:59,243
So again, at the end of the day, the format that works best for you is gonna be the one that is number one, most accessible to you, but number two is the one that you enjoy reading.

98
00:08:59,243 --> 00:09:05,763
So hopefully this has broken down a few of the different potential technological advantages, format advantages and focus advantages.

99
00:09:05,763 --> 00:09:10,730
But again, ultimately, the kind of format that you enjoy the most is going to be the best one the majority of the time.

100
00:09:10,730 --> 00:09:24,882
It's also worth noting that the format of the books you read really isn't the most important factor to reading more often, reading more deeply, that is building a reading habit, which in my experience is pretty easy to fall out of, but it's also possible to get back into as well.

101
00:09:24,882 --> 00:09:28,359
One useful tactic that I've used in the past is a 90-day reading challenge.

102
00:09:28,359 --> 00:09:32,883
Essentially, I told my friend Martin, "Hey, I'm gonna read nonfiction every single day for the next 90 days."

103
00:09:32,883 --> 00:09:34,704
If I don't, I'm gonna pay you 100 bucks.

104
00:09:34,704 --> 00:09:39,299
And I gave him a spreadsheet where I tracked all my progress and he could follow along every single day.

105
00:09:39,299 --> 00:09:40,762
And that was a very successful challenge.

106
00:09:40,762 --> 00:09:47,712
I read 11 books during those three months, many of which I reference all the time on this channel and actually built my reading habit long-term after that.

107
00:09:47,712 --> 00:09:53,428
And there are a lot of other tricks you can use for building long-term habits, whether it's reading more books or doing anything else you wanna do.

